## Data Storage Format

Contacts are stored in `contacts.txt` as JSON objects, one per line:

```json
{"name": "John Doe", "phone": "(123) 456-7890", "email": "john@example.com", "address": "123 Main St, City, State 12345"}
{"name": "Jane Smith", "phone": "098-765-4321", "email": "jane@example.com", "address": "456 Oak Ave, City, State 67890"}
```

## Input Validation

### Phone Number
Valid formats:
- `1234567890`
- `123-456-7890`
- `(123) 456-7890`
- `+1234567890`

### Email
Must follow standard email format:
- `user@example.com`
- `john.doe@company.co.uk`

### Name
- Minimum 2 characters
- Cannot be empty

## Features in Detail

### Add Contact
- Validates all inputs before adding
- Prevents duplicate contacts (same name and phone)
- Stores in sorted order by name
- Automatic file persistence

### View All Contacts
- Displays all stored contacts
- Automatically sorted by name (case-insensitive)
- Shows complete details for each contact

### Search Contact
- Search by full or partial name (case-insensitive)
- Search by partial or full phone number
- Returns all matching contacts

### Update Contact
- Search for contact to update
- Shows current details
- Allows selective field updates (press Enter to keep existing)
- Validates new input before saving

### Delete Contact
- Confirmation required before deletion
- Handles multiple matches gracefully
- Permanent removal from file

